later George with a train to catch Jacob  Evie agreed like you fragrance  Assassin's Creed syndicate is a return  to form for the storied franchise  delivering a unique engaging tale in an  open world that truly feels alive not  just in being bolts and dashes miss  Wright human voice the game feels closer  to 2009 s Assassin's Creed 2 than it  does the more recent titles because it  focuses on elements that made those  early games so soulful lovable  protagonists interesting missions with  intriguing characters a gorgeous setting  and traversal abilities that just feel  really good  the story follows twins jacob and evie  frye through 1868 london while Evie is  focused on finding a magical artifact a  piece of Eden hidden somewhere in the  city Jacob is busy building a street  gang and saving London's downtrodden  from injustice along the way they make  friends with the likes of Alexander  Graham Bell Charles Dickens and Karl  Marx just to name a few historical  figures and all these people assist them  in their mission to dismantle the city's  Templar conspiracy and protect the  little people  not only are their personalities  different so are their skills evey  special skills are stealth focused  including the incredibly useful  chameleon ability that causes her to  disappear completely while she's  standing still in sneak mode her stealth  stats far exceed her brothers so she's  your girl for bounty hunting and  liberation missions Jacob on the other  hand is a brawler more suited to  fisticuffs he takes more damage and can  bring enemies to near-death states  quicker their differences are noticeable  in gameplay and rather than have one  character you can customize either way  it's a nice convenient touch to have two  characters ready and available for  different goals  I love Jacob and Evie  the banter between these two is a  constant joy and their interpersonal  conflict is as enjoyable to watch unfold  as their external one she's serious and  tough but sweet and he's totally snarky  and a little goofy but their duality  both in the field and with one another  makes for an engaging story with some  intimate touches I find any more wild  geese for you to chase I'll be touch of  more pleasant for your absence  combat is fluid and training combos for  devastating execution moves is simple to  achieve it's easy to adapt strategy mid  battle switching to pistols and smoke  bombs in a hot second when simple blades  won't do most fights play out like a  brutal beautiful dance flying between  enemies in a gang war a brawl involving  as many as a dozen and a half combatants  on both you and the enemy side is  undeniably satisfying freerunning  follows the same simplicity you can  climb pretty much everything with  relative ease  but controls take some time getting used  to and occasionally feel  counterintuitive sometimes if you want  to climb up your character will fly away  diagonally as though they are running on  a grid so some spaces halt free running  although it happens infrequently it can  be frustrating  as for stealth you can snap in and out  at any time as you prowl the streets  nearby enemy locations and how much they  notice you are shown via a threat ring  this tool makes stealth much easier and  allows you to gauge who to take out and  when and you have a ton of options  smoke bomb them and take out the leader  escape to a rooftop and pick them off  one by one with throwing knives or make  them turn on each other with  hallucinogenic darts how you combine  your tools is up to your whims and  syndicates mission design as well as the  organic way the world lets you slip in  and out of missions gives you ample  breathing room to complete each one in  your own way I almost never use  syndicates fast travel points because  with the rope launcher why would you  take any other route through London you  become a victorian-era spider-man with  the Rope launcher you can set up air  assassinations travel across large gaps  between buildings zipline around and  quickly escape from a fight it's my  favorite item in the entire game  another new mechanic in the mix is the  systemic vehicle system carriage driving  is relatively easy to handle you can use  them for a number of things including  but not limited to hijacking them to  race down a target kidnapping hiding  corpses destroying enemies carriages or  for a simple afternoon ride with your  gang you can also get into fights on top  of them it's all very Grand Theft Auto  you even get experience point phone is's  for destroying public property easy you  can also unlock perks for your gang the  rooks I was obsessed with tricking them  out because having strong guys on the  streets meant I always had backup  in-game currency can purchase your gang  things like more well-made carriages and  give your rivals worse ones as well as  cheap access to medicine and darts you  can even pay off policemen to turn a  blind eye to your illegal activities and  assemble an army of children to collect  crafting items for you micromanaging  your gang is worthwhile because it  completely changes the social landscape  of London you have an active role in  ensuring your people are where you need  them to be when you need them to be  there and have the best tools pounds can  buy syndicates story is heartfelt and  exciting but its final boss fight is  poorly set up it tosses narrative  tension out the window and left me a  little bit disappointed but a  disappointing final fight and some  control hitches cannot diminish  Assassin's Creed syndicate charm it puts  the franchise back in touch with its  roots and presents a wonderful tale full  of heart ziplining through London is  thrilling and the organic unfolding of  missions with open-ended solutions  allows for a meaningful deeply personal  experience level believes an excellent  mission design make a sake creed  syndicate a treasure of gameplay and  storytelling  you
 

